# Copy this file to .env and paste your key in.
#
#   macOS / Linux:  cp .env.example .env
#   Windows:        copy .env.example .env
#
# SAME KEY YOU SET UP IN UNIT 1. Nothing new to sign up for.
#
# .env is listed in .gitignore, so it never reaches GitHub. Never paste your
# key into your code, your README, a screenshot, or a message. If you think
# you've exposed it, say so in the help channel and delete the key — keys are
# free to replace and nobody is in trouble for it.
#
# If you need a new one: https://aistudio.google.com — sign in with a Google
# account, CREATE YOUR OWN PROJECT when prompted, and make a key inside it.

GEMINI_API_KEY=your_key_here

# ─── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Turn the response cache off entirely (run_eval.py already does this).
# AI201_CACHE=0

# ─── feature/full-ui branch only ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Login, saved search history, a persisted wardrobe, and favorites. Not used
# by main or by anything graded — webapp.py refuses to start without these.
#
# Get these from your Supabase project: Settings -> API.
#   SUPABASE_URL                your project URL, e.g. https://xxxx.supabase.co
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY   the "service_role" secret key, NOT the "anon"
#                               key — webapp.py is the only thing that ever
#                               talks to Supabase (no browser-side client), so
#                               it needs the key that can read/write any row,
#                               and does its own per-user filtering in code.
#                               Treat it like GEMINI_API_KEY: never commit it,
#                               never paste it anywhere public.
#
# Also run supabase/schema.sql once, in your Supabase project's SQL editor,
# before starting webapp.py for the first time.

# SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_service_role_key_here

# Signs Flask's session cookie (who's logged in). Any random string; changing
# it logs everyone out. If unset, webapp.py generates one at startup and warns
# — fine for local testing, but it means a restart logs everyone out.
# FLASK_SECRET_KEY=change_this_to_something_random
